 Here, I'll convert the term with base 5 to the term with base 125 in order to have the same residual term on both sides of the equation.
$5^{x + 4} = \bf {5^{3x}} {/eq}
Now, take the logarithm of both sides to remove the residual exponent term.
{eq}\bf \log_5(5^{x+ 4}) = \log_5(5^{3x}) {/eq}
By property of logarithm, the base and the inside of logarithmic term will cancel out.
Now, simplify the remaining terms.
$$x + 4 = 3x\\ x - 3x = -4\\ \bf{-2x = -4}\\ \bf{x = 2} {/eq}
Answer: By applying the power of 2 to both sides of the equation, we have solved for the residual x. $$5^{2 + 4} = 125^2\\ \bf{5^6 = 15625} {/eq}

 Solution $:$ By definition, $\cos \theta = \dfrac{\langle n_1,n_2 \rangle}{|n_1||n_2|}$ where $n_1$ and $n_2$ are the normal vectors to planes $2x + y - 2z + 3 = 0$ and $6x + 3y + 2z - 5 = 0$ respectively. \begin{align}
\cos \theta &= \dfrac{\langle 2,1,-2 \rangle \bullet \langle 6,3,2 \rangle}{\sqrt{2^2+1^2+(-2)^2} \sqrt{6^2+3^2+4^2}} \ \ \ \left[ \because n_1 = (2,1,-2) , n_2 =(6,3,2) \right] 
\\ & = \dfrac{\langle 2 \bullet 6 + 1 \bullet 3 + (-2) \bullet 2 \rangle}{\sqrt{4 + 1 + 4} \sqrt{36 + 9 + 16}}
\\ \cos \theta &= \dfrac 8{\sqrt{145}}.
\end{align} </think> $\dfrac 8{\sqrt{145}}$

